<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle Hymn of the Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written the night of 18 November <a href="/years/1861">1861</a>; first published February <a href="/years/1862">1862</a> in The Atlantic Monthly</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The tune came from a folk hymn of the revivalist camp meetings of the late 1700s, &quot;Say, Brothers will you Meet Us&quot;, first published in the early 1800s; its first known printed form was &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>'s Happy Shore&quot;. By the 1850s it carried the &quot;Glory, glory, hallelujah&quot; chorus, and the tune spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div>
